<h2 id="browser-recovery">Keep a recovery copy on this browser</h2>
<p>Browser recovery is optional and off by default in the public writing tools. Open Keep work on this browser, acknowledge its private contents, and choose Enable browser recovery. After an edit, a recovery copy is saved after a short pause; Save recovery now requests an immediate save. Check the status before leaving. The private AI pilot does not offer this feature.</p>
<p>One unencrypted workspace, up to 512 KiB, is kept in this site's local browser storage. It includes article fields, source notes, and saved review questions. It excludes pending imports and clipboard source, unsaved image/link/table/passage preparation, revision history, collections, approval, export choices, and credentials. Apply or save preparation to make it ordinary workspace writing before relying on recovery.</p>
<ol><li>On returning, the tool offers the saved copy without opening its contents automatically.</li><li>Acknowledge storage again, then choose Restore copy and resume saving. If you already have writing, confirm replacement. Previous valid writing is checkpointed first. Restore clears approval.</li><li>Only one tab can save at a time. Pause saving in the other tab, or close it, before resuming here. A saved copy cannot be silently overwritten by a different workspace.</li></ol>
<p>Pause saving keeps the existing copy. Remove browser copy asks before removing it and leaves open writing and downloaded files unchanged. Clear this writing desk also asks before removing the stored browser copy; another active saving tab blocks removal. Saving stops after leaving and must be enabled again after reopening. An external storage change stops the writer.</p>
<p>This is not encrypted storage, account history, cross-device sync, or a guaranteed backup. Anyone using the same browser profile may restore the copy. Browser settings, private browsing, eviction, or device problems can remove it. If storage is full, blocked, or a write fails, saving stops and the last successfully stored copy is kept where possible. Unsupported browsers use private backup files instead. Keep a downloaded private backup for important work.</p>
